<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip V of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/238-bce">238</a>–<a href="/years/179-bce">179 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was Antigonid king of Macedonia from 221 to 179 BCE. He expanded Macedonian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bylazora">Bylazora</a> in Paeonia. His siege of Abydos in 200 BCE helped bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>, and his defeat at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE confined him to Macedonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip V of Macedon" width="320" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Philip_V_of_Macedon_in_Palazzo_Massimo_(Rome).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-aetolicus">Demetrius II of Macedon</a>. He was nine years old when his father died in 229 BCE, and his great-uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> administered the kingdom as regent and then as king until his own death in 221 BCE, when Philip was seventeen. As a young man he was attractive and charismatic, compared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> he became a universal favourite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> because of his benevolent conduct towards them. He had a close relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a>, who held a prominent position at his court until 213 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 221 BCE Philip took over the leadership of the Hellenic League, which Antigonus Doson had assembled at Corinth, and led it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-220-217-bce">Social War</a> of 220 to 217 BCE against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and Elis. After the sack of Thermum, the Aetolian political and religious capital, the war ended at the conference of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a> in 217 BCE. In 215 BCE he signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal Barca</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> in 211 BCE weakened his position, but he concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-phoenice">Peace of Phoenice</a> in 205 BCE.</p><p>Philip then agreed with the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> to take Egyptian-held territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and capturing Miletus. Pergamum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> were alarmed and appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. While Philip besieged Abydos in the autumn of 200 BCE, a Roman ambassador, Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, urged him not to attack Greek states, but in vain. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a> he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</a> at the Battle of the Aous in 198 BCE and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a> in June 197 BCE. In 196 BCE he gave up his claim to all Greek cities on the mainland and in Asia Minor, including Corinth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>, the so-called fetters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the peace Philip cooperated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, supporting it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis of Sparta</a> in 195 BCE and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> let him keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> and other territory he had conquered. He turned to rebuilding Macedonia, imposing taxes, exploiting royal property, reopening mines and minting a new currency.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> stayed suspicious, and accusations from neighbouring states led to constant interference. His younger son Demetrius, encouraged by Rome to think of the succession, quarrelled with his elder brother Perseus, and Philip reluctantly had Demetrius executed for treason in 180 BCE. He died at Amphipolis towards the end of 179 BCE and was succeeded by Perseus, the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip V of Macedon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war split Gortyn into rival factions and drew in <b>Philip V of Macedon</b> and the Achaean League on the side of Knossos' opponents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Against <b>Philip V of Macedon</b> it fought at Chios and Lade in 201 BCE, and in the war against Antiochus III its fleet under Eudamus defeated a Seleucid squadron commanded by Hannibal off Side in 190 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad, Antiochus III and <b>Philip V of Macedon</b> had agreed to divide Egypt's overseas possessions, and the Battle of Panium in 198 BCE had cost the Ptolemies Coele-Syria.</blockquote><a href="/work/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 200 BCE <b>Philip V</b> of the Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia besieged Abydos; rather than surrender, many citizens killed their families and themselves.</blockquote><a href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a></li><li><blockquote>Leaders such as Dorimachus and Scopas fought <b>Philip V of Macedon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a></li><li><blockquote>A common body of Cretans (koinon) appears in the 3rd century BCE, and around 216 BCE <b>Philip V of Macedon</b> was recognised as its protector (prostates), seeking influence over the island and its mercenaries.</blockquote><a href="/state/cretan-koinon">Cretan Koinon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Amphipolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.82,23.85&amp;year=-200" aria-label="Amphipolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5134 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Philip V of Macedon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.82,23.85&amp;year=-200">What was here in 200 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
